# Configuration for myMPD, read by /etc/init.d/S96mympd.
#
# myMPD does not have a config file. It keeps one small file per setting
# under <workdir>/config, and on every start it reads the environment
# first: if MYMPD_<SETTING> is set, the matching file is deleted and
# rewritten from the environment. So anything exported here wins over
# whatever is in the work directory, on every boot.
#
# The work directory is on /data, so settings changed in the web UI do
# survive a reboot - but anything exported here is reapplied on every
# start and overwrites them. Export a setting to pin it; leave it out to
# let the web UI own it.
#
# The full list of settings is in myMPD's
# docs/020-configuration/configuration-files.rst.
#
# Hence the two kinds of variable below. The exported ones are myMPD's
# own and are named MYMPD_<setting>; the unexported ones are read by the
# init script and mean nothing to the binary. myMPD has no config item
# called workdir, cachedir, syslog or args, so the two namespaces do not
# collide.

# Settings, the album cache and state files go on the data partition.
# The disk caches do not: they are regenerable, they are the only thing
# here that grows without a bound, and writing cover art to a CF card
# continuously is how the card dies.
#
# Both of these are myMPD's own compiled-in defaults, deliberately.
# /var/lib/mympd is a symlink to /data/mympd, and /var/cache is a
# skeleton symlink to /tmp, so the paths below and the paths a bare
# "mympd" with no arguments would use are the same places. Before that
# symlink existed, running "mympd" by hand on the unit produced forty
# lines of "Read-only file system" pointing at /var/lib/mympd - nowhere
# near where the service had been told to write, and a fine way to send
# whoever is debugging in the wrong direction.
MYMPD_WORKDIR="/var/lib/mympd"
MYMPD_CACHEDIR="/var/cache/mympd"

# Plain HTTP on port 80. This is a LAN appliance and the image already
# runs telnetd, so TLS here would be a fig leaf; it is off for a more
# concrete reason as well. myMPD generates a self-signed certificate
# into its work directory on first start, that work directory is a
# tmpfs, and RSA keygen on a 500 MHz Geode is not something to do on
# every boot for a certificate the browser will reject anyway.
export MYMPD_HTTP=true
export MYMPD_HTTP_PORT=80
export MYMPD_SSL=false

# Disk caches off. 0 disables a cache, -1 would keep it forever. The
# cache directory is a tmpfs, so these live in the 256 MB of RAM the
# unit has. Turning them off costs re-reading artwork from the files
# each time it is displayed.
export MYMPD_CACHE_COVER_KEEP_DAYS=0
export MYMPD_CACHE_THUMBS_KEEP_DAYS=0
export MYMPD_CACHE_LYRICS_KEEP_DAYS=0
export MYMPD_CACHE_HTTP_KEEP_DAYS=0

# Log to syslog rather than to a file of our own. syslogd is already
# running by the time this starts and writes to /var/log, which is a
# symlink to /tmp.
MYMPD_SYSLOG=1

# No outbound HTTPS. This image ships no CA certificate bundle, because
# the proposal argues against trusting the public CA system with a frozen
# bundle that expires years after anyone is thinking about it. Anything
# reaching out over TLS therefore cannot verify a peer and should not be
# trying. WebradioDB is the one feature that phones home by itself; the
# script-driven ones - lyrics fetching, ListenBrainz, fanart - cannot run
# at all, because Lua is compiled out.
#
# cert_check=false is what allows myMPD to start at all on this image,
# and it is not the security decision it looks like.
#
# mympd_read_ca_certificates() in src/lib/config/cacertstore.c returns
# false when cert_check is on and there is no CA store, and main.c treats
# that as a fatal startup error. On a BDP-1 myMPD got all the way to
# "Listening on http://0.0.0.0:80" and then exited with:
#
#   EMERG mympd: System certificate store not found.
#   NOTICE mympd: Exiting erroneous, thank you for using myMPD
#
# The check short-circuits on cert_check == false, before it looks for a
# store. What is being switched off is certificate verification for
# *outbound* connections, and this image makes no outbound TLS
# connections: webradiodb is off below, and the script-driven features
# that would fetch lyrics, ListenBrainz data or fanart cannot run because
# Lua is compiled out. So this disables verification of connections that
# do not happen. If outbound HTTPS is ever wanted, the answer is to point
# ca_cert_store at a pinned certificate, not to ship a public CA bundle -
# which is the proposal's position and Phase 4's job.
export MYMPD_CERT_CHECK=false

# There is a second, separate "EMERG CA cert store not found." logged
# once per start, from the code that computes the *default* value of
# ca_cert_store. That one is harmless and cannot be silenced: the lookup
# runs before the environment is read, unconditionally.
export MYMPD_WEBRADIODB=false

# Uncomment to restrict the web UI to a subnet. Empty means no
# restriction. The format is myMPD's own, not CIDR-only:
#export MYMPD_ACL="+192.168.0.0/16"

# Extra arguments, if any are ever needed. Work and cache directories
# are passed by the init script from the two variables above.
MYMPD_ARGS=""
